In a non-strict function with a simple parameter list, `arguments[i]` aliases parameter `i`. When a `var` or function declaration redeclares the parameter, the minifier tracks it like an ordinary local and removes or inlines writes that are still visible through `arguments`.

Parameters of these functions are now marked as observable, so unused-code removal and single-use inlining keep their writes. The marking does not depend on the `unused` option, because single-use inlining also runs with `compress: { unused: false }`.

Script sources now always carry this observability state. Without it, `unused: false` inlined a root-level `var` nested in a statement: `try { var x = foo(); throw x } catch {}` became `try { throw foo(); } catch {}`, which removes a global that later scripts can read.

The guard is conservative: it covers every sloppy function with a simple parameter list, whether or not it uses `arguments`. #27525 narrows it to functions that can reach their `arguments` object.

## Example

```js
(function (a) {
  var a;
  console.log(a = "foo", arguments[0]);
})("bar");
```

This prints `foo foo`. Before, the minified code printed `foo bar`:

```js
(function(a) {
	console.log("foo", arguments[0]);
})("bar");
```

After, the write is kept:

```js
(function(a) {
	var a;
	console.log(a = "foo", arguments[0]);
})("bar");
```

The Rust regressions cover Script and CommonJS with unused-code removal on and off. The NAPI test runs the source and the minified output in `node:vm`
